<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Welser family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of Welser</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Welser were a family of merchants and bankers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, among the richest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the 16th century alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugger-family">Fuggers</a>. They traded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, textiles and metals from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and lent large sums to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. In return, in 1528 he granted them the right to colonise and govern the province of Venezuela, the colony known as Klein-Venedig, which they held until the 1540s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/welser-family.jpg" alt="Welser family" width="320" height="511" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Welser_family.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
